# React Module Anti-Patterns

**Trigger**: Auto-applied to all `.tsx` files in `components/` and `app/`.

## Third-Party UI Library Import Anti-Pattern

Never import from bare package names that export types only. This is the most common cause of `"Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useContext')"` when using Radix UI, shadcn/ui, or similar libraries.

### The Problem

`radix-ui` is a **types-only metapackage**. It re-exports TypeScript types for documentation purposes but does **not** include real React components. When you import from it:

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — radix-ui has no real components, no React context
import { Tooltip, TooltipTrigger } from "radix-ui";
```

The imported values are bare module exports with no React component implementation. When Radix's internal `useContext` calls try to access their provider context, they find `null` because no real component tree was established.

### The Fix

Always import from the actual `@radix-ui/react-*` packages:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — real component packages with full React context
import { Tooltip, TooltipTrigger, TooltipContent, TooltipProvider } from "@radix-ui/react-tooltip";
import { Dialog, DialogTrigger, DialogContent, DialogTitle } from "@radix-ui/react-dialog";
import { Select, SelectTrigger, SelectValue, SelectContent, SelectItem } from "@radix-ui/react-select";
import { DropdownMenu, DropdownMenuTrigger, DropdownMenuContent, DropdownMenuItem } from "@radix-ui/react-dropdown-menu";
import { Switch } from "@radix-ui/react-switch";
import { Tabs, TabsList, TabsTrigger, TabsContent } from "@radix-ui/react-tabs";
import { Accordion, AccordionItem, AccordionTrigger, AccordionContent } from "@radix-ui/react-accordion";
import { Progress } from "@radix-ui/react-progress";
import { Separator } from "@radix-ui/react-separator";
import { Avatar, AvatarImage, AvatarFallback } from "@radix-ui/react-avatar";
import { Label } from "@radix-ui/react-label";
```

### Detection

| Pattern | Tool | Check |
|---------|------|-------|
| Import from `"radix-ui"` | Grep/ripgrep | `grep -r 'from "radix-ui"'` |
| Import from `'radix-ui'` | Grep/ripgrep | `grep -r "from 'radix-ui'"` |
| UI components crashing with `useContext` null | Runtime | Check for wrong import source |

### Shadcn/ui Convention

All shadcn/ui components in `@/components/ui/` must use real `@radix-ui/react-*` imports internally. When adding new components via `npx shadcn@latest add`, verify the generated imports.

## Module-Level JSX is Forbidden

Never create JSX elements at the module scope. This causes hydration errors in Server Components and prevents tree-shaking.

### The Anti-Pattern

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — JSX at module scope (evaluated during module load)
const statusConfig = {
  PUBLISHED: { label: "Published", icon: <CheckCircle weight="bold" /> },
  FAILED: { label: "Failed", icon: <WarningCircle weight="bold" /> },
};

function Component() {
  return <div>{statusConfig[status].icon}</div>;
}
```

**Why it breaks**: Module-level JSX is evaluated when the file is first imported, not during render. In Server Components this causes "Context mismatch" hydration errors because React tree context isn't established yet. In Client Components it prevents proper hook context propagation.

### The Fix

**Option A: Move JSX inside the component function**

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — JSX created during render
function Component({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const config: Record<string, { label: string; icon: React.ReactNode }> = {
    PUBLISHED: { label: "Published", icon: <CheckCircle weight="bold" /> },
    FAILED: { label: "Failed", icon: <WarningCircle weight="bold" /> },
  };
  return <div>{config[status].icon}</div>;
}
```

**Option B: Store icon names/names, not JSX, at module scope**

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — plain data at module scope, JSX during render
const statusConfig: Record<string, { label: string; icon: string }> = {
  PUBLISHED: { label: "Published", icon: "CheckCircle" },
  FAILED: { label: "Failed", icon: "WarningCircle" },
};

function Component({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const cfg = statusConfig[status];
  return <div>{renderIcon(cfg.icon)} {cfg.label}</div>;
}
```

**Option C: Store a render function, not JSX**

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — function returns JSX, not JSX itself
const statusConfig: Record<string, { label: string; icon: () => React.ReactNode }> = {
  PUBLISHED: { label: "Published", icon: () => <CheckCircle weight="bold" /> },
  FAILED: { label: "Failed", icon: () => <WarningCircle weight="bold" /> },
};

function Component({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const cfg = statusConfig[status];
  return <div>{cfg.icon()} {cfg.label}</div>;
}
```

## Module-Level Safe Patterns

These are **always safe** at module scope:

| Pattern | Example | Why Safe |
|---------|---------|----------|
| Plain data objects | `const config = { key: "value" }` | No JSX, no React |
| Type definitions | `interface Props { name: string }` | Compile-time only |
| Const strings/numbers | `const MAX_SIZE = 100` | Primitive values |
| Import statements | `import { Card } from "..."` | Standard module loading |
| Utility functions | `function format(n: number) { ... }` | No side effects |
| Icon component references | `import { Sparkle } from "..."; const icon = Sparkle` | References, not JSX |

## Configuration Objects

When building status/confidence/config maps, use data-only values at module scope:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — module-level config with plain data
const statusConfig: Record<string, { label: string; variant: "default" | "destructive" }> = {
  PUBLISHED: { label: "Published", variant: "default" },
  FAILED: { label: "Failed", variant: "destructive" },
};

// Then in the component:
function StatusBadge({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const cfg = statusConfig[status];
  return <Badge variant={cfg.variant}>{cfg.label}</Badge>;
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — icon as React component type at module scope
import type { Icon } from "@phosphor-icons/react";
import { CheckCircle, WarningCircle } from "@phosphor-icons/react";

const statusIcons: Record<string, Icon> = {
  PUBLISHED: CheckCircle,
  FAILED: WarningCircle,
};

function StatusBadge({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const IconComponent = statusIcons[status];
  return <IconComponent className="size-4" weight="bold" />;
}
```

## Type Safety

### No `Record<string, unknown>` for Known Shapes

When the shape is known, define an interface:

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — loses type safety
const audience: Record<string, unknown> = {
  interests: ["tech", "design"],
};
const interests = audience.interests; // Error: unknown

// ✅ GOOD — typed
interface Audience {
  interests: string[];
}
const audience: Audience = {
  interests: ["tech", "design"],
};
```

### No `as any` Casting

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
const user = session.user as any;

// ✅ GOOD
const user = session.user as { name: string; workspaceId: string };

// ✅ BETTER — define a type
interface SessionUser {
  name: string;
  workspaceId: string;
}
const user = session.user as SessionUser;
```

### Exhaustive Switch/Map Guards

When mapping from string keys to components or config, always provide a fallback:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — has fallback
const cfg = statusConfig[status] ?? statusConfig.DRAFT;
const IconComponent = statusIcons[name] ?? DefaultIcon;
```

## Performance: Expensive Computations in Render

Avoid expensive operations in the component body. Use `useMemo` in Client Components:

```tsx
// Client Component
"use client";
import { useMemo } from "react";

export function CalendarPreview({ posts }: { posts: ScheduledPost[] }) {
  const days = useMemo(() => {
    // expensive computation: filtering, date parsing, grouping
    return computeDays(posts);
  }, [posts]);

  return <div>{days.map(...)}</div>;
}
```

In Server Components, compute before the JSX return:

```tsx
// Server Component
export default async function Page() {
  const data = await computeExpensiveData(); // runs once on server
  return <Display data={data} />;
}
```

## CSS Variable Token Enforcement

Never hardcode colors. All colors must use CSS variable tokens via Tailwind classes:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — semantic tokens
<Badge className="bg-success/10 text-success" />
<span className="text-muted-foreground" />
<div className="border-border" />

// ❌ BAD — hardcoded
<Badge style={{ backgroundColor: "#22c55e" }} />
<span style={{ color: "#888" }} />
```

Allowed exceptions:
- `next.config.ts` configuration
- Build-time scripts (`prisma/seed.ts`, `test-*.ts`)
- Test files

## Verification

Before committing changes:

- [ ] No JSX expressions at module scope (outside component functions)
- [ ] No `<Icon />` JSX inside const objects at module level
- [ ] No `as any` casts (use proper types)
- [ ] No hardcoded hex/rgb color values in components
- [ ] Configuration objects use plain data, not JSX
- [ ] Icon references stored as component types, not rendered elements
- [ ] `pnpm run typecheck` passes (0 errors)
- [ ] `pnpm run build` passes (no hydration errors)
